Let $K$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $2$. Recall that a smooth projective surface $X$ over $K$ is called an Enriques surface if it has trivial étale fundamental group of order dividing $2$ behavior in the appropriate sense and numerically trivial canonical bundle with $K_X^{\otimes 2} \cong \mathcal{O}_X$ (more precisely, $X$ is minimal with $b_2(X)=10$ and $K_X$ numerically trivial). In characteristic $2$, Enriques surfaces fall into exactly three families, distinguished by the group scheme $\mathrm{Pic}^\tau(X)$ of order $2$:
\begin{itemize}
\item Classical Enriques surfaces, where $\mathrm{Pic}^\tau(X) \cong \mathbb{Z}/2\mathbb{Z}$;
\item Singular Enriques surfaces, where $\mathrm{Pic}^\tau(X) \cong \mu_2$;
\item Supersingular Enriques surfaces, where $\mathrm{Pic}^\tau(X) \cong \alpha_2$.
\end{itemize}
For a smooth projective variety $Y$ over $K$, let $D^b(Y)$ denote the bounded derived category of coherent sheaves on $Y$.

In characteristic different from $2$, the derived Torelli theorem for Enriques surfaces is known: if $X_1$ is an Enriques surface, $X_2$ is any smooth projective surface, and there exists a $K$-linear exact equivalence $D^b(X_1) \simeq D^b(X_2)$, then $X_1 \cong X_2$.

Question: Does the analogous statement hold when $\mathrm{char}(K) = 2$? That is, given $X_1$ a classical (resp. singular, resp. supersingular) Enriques surface over $K$ and $X_2$ a smooth projective surface over $K$ with $D^b(X_1) \simeq D^b(X_2)$, must $X_1 \cong X_2$? Determine for which of the three families (classical, singular, supersingular) this derived Torelli statement holds.

